INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-08-18', '## Lok Sabha — 18 August 2025

The sitting opened with **Question Hour** featuring a wide range of policy questions. Members raised concerns about the **PM Internship Scheme**, noting that over six lakh applications were received in Round I but only eight thousand opportunities were created — with Tier-II cities and smaller districts like Nandyal severely underserved.

**Education** featured prominently: questions probed funds allocated under the digital education initiatives and **AI Centres of Excellence** in healthcare, agriculture, and sustainable cities. Members sought details on implementation timelines and utilisation certificates.

**Labour and employment** was another focus area, with questions on **Model Career Centres (MCCs)** under the National Career Service project, particularly their reach in Tamil Nadu.

**Sports and youth affairs** drew attention, with debate on India''s bids to host the **2030 Commonwealth Games** and **2036 Olympics**, and on a proposed National Youth Mental Health Awareness Policy.

**Corporate affairs** questions addressed the MCA portal Version 3 upgrade and its impact on business compliance.

> "In Round I, more than six lakh applications were received, only eight thousand opportunities were given — the Tier-II cities need attention." — Dr. Byreddy Shabari', '["PM Internship Scheme", "AI Centres of Excellence", "Model Career Centres", "Olympics bid", "Youth Mental Health"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-08-19', '## Lok Sabha — 19 August 2025

**Question Hour** dominated proceedings with a wide sweep of policy domains. Members pressed the government on **PMAY (housing)** for Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, and women, seeking data on allocations and waiting lists.

The **Production Linked Incentive (PLI) scheme** came under scrutiny, with questions on sector-wise fund utilisation and measurable impact on domestic manufacturing.

**Agriculture exports** were debated extensively — APEDA and MPEDA promotional activities, export of south Indian small onions, and support for farmer-exporters.

**Fisheries and dairy** questions covered the PM Matsya Sampada Yojana, with members seeking data on women beneficiaries in the sector.

The **automobile industry** and Chinese electric motors were flagged: members asked whether Chinese-made motors were being used to skirt export curbs while still claiming PLI subsidies.

**Skills and welfare** featured questions on PM-DAKSH Yojana training for marginalised groups.

> "Whether the Government has allowed the automobile firms to use fully built Chinese-made motors in their vehicles to skirt China''s magnet export curbs in order to continue availing Government Subsidies" — Shri Manish Jaiswal', '["PLI Scheme", "PMAY housing", "Agriculture exports", "PM Matsya Sampada", "Chinese auto components"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-08-20', '## Lok Sabha — 20 August 2025

The sitting saw substantive debate on **disaster management**, with members asking about the effectiveness of current strategies for timely communication during emergencies.

**Nuclear medicine and cancer** were raised under PM questions — members sought details on the Department of Atomic Energy''s contribution to cancer treatment and research, including advanced radiotherapy infrastructure.

**Atal Tinkering Labs (ATLs)** under the Atal Innovation Mission were examined, with members from Andhra Pradesh seeking State-specific progress on lab sanctioning, establishment, and operational status.

**Railways** featured in multiple questions: proposed Theni–Erumeli broad gauge line, redevelopment of stations under the **Amrit Bharat Station Scheme (ABSS)** in Tripura and Bihar, and connectivity gaps in tribal areas.

**Legal Metrology** came up with questions about the centralised online platform for streamlining registration and compliance.

**Renewable Energy** questions focused on research and innovation in solar, wind, and hydrogen storage, and the institutions driving next-generation energy technology.

**Minority Affairs** debated the UMEED Central Portal for Waqf management transparency.

> "What are the focus areas for next-generation energy technology under the Ministry''s research mandate?" — Shri Naresh Ganpat Mhaske', '["Disaster Management", "Cancer Treatment", "Atal Tinkering Labs", "Amrit Bharat Station Scheme", "Renewable Energy Research"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-08-21', '## Lok Sabha — 21 August 2025

**Road safety and infrastructure** led the day''s questions, with members raising road accident data, pothole-related fatalities, and the government''s asset monetisation strategy for road infrastructure.

**MSME and global value chains** were debated — members from Tamil Nadu pressed the government on schemes to integrate small enterprises into international trade, including single-window access and export promotion.

**Civil Aviation** saw a proposal for renaming **Coimbatore International Airport**, with members seeking government response.

**Tribal welfare** featured questions on expanding the **PM Janjatiya Gram Abhiyan (PM-JUGA)** to more villages, with implementation data sought.

**Natural gas** policy was examined — members questioned whether India remains on track to raise natural gas to **15% of the energy mix by 2030**.

**Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) Phase-II** was assessed, with questions on ODF Plus village verification and fund utilisation.

> "Whether the Government is aware of the need of improving integration of MSMEs with global value chains" — Sushri S. Jothimani', '["Road Safety", "MSME Global Value Chains", "Coimbatore Airport", "PM Janjatiya Gram Abhiyan", "Natural Gas Policy"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-12-01', '## Lok Sabha — 1 December 2025

The House opened the **Winter Session 2025** on a celebratory note — the Speaker extended congratulations to the **Indian Women Cricket Team** for winning the Women Cricket World Cup 2025, and to the **Indian Blind Women Cricket Team** for winning the inaugural Women''s T20 Cricket World Cup for the Blind.

**Corporate affairs** questions examined the **Niveshak Didi Phase II** initiative and measures to strengthen financial literacy and investor outreach.

**Employment law** drew debate: members questioned whether the government plans to raise the EPF salary ceiling to ₹30,000, with particular attention to impact on unorganised sector workers.

**LIC investments in Adani Group** were raised, with members from the opposition pressing for full disclosure of the total investment made amid ongoing regulatory scrutiny.

**Education** questions highlighted the number of government schools with fewer than ten enrolled students — and the fiscal paradox of teachers drawing salaries in near-empty schools.

**IDBI Bank disinvestment** was questioned, with members seeking timelines and strategic rationale.

**Unemployment data** entered debate: with national unemployment edging up to 5.2 per cent in September 2025, members asked for disaggregated State-level analysis and policy responses.

> "Whether LIC has recently made investment into the Adani Group despite ongoing regulatory scrutiny" — Sushri Mahua Moitra', '["LIC Adani Investment", "EPF Salary Ceiling", "IDBI Disinvestment", "School Enrolment Crisis", "Unemployment Rate"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-12-02', '## Lok Sabha — 2 December 2025

**Rural development** dominated the sitting, with multiple questions on **MGNREGS** wage arrears owed to States and progress under **PMGSY** road construction — members sought State-wise and district-wise breakdowns of new road construction and existing road upgrades.

**Startups** came under scrutiny, with debate on DPIIT-recognised startups, tax exemptions under Startup India, and whether support had meaningfully scaled to non-metro entrepreneurs.

**Persons with Disabilities** welfare measures were discussed, including integrative rehabilitation approaches and their geographic reach.

**Gati Shakti** platform questions probed the unified geospatial interface — its datasets, services, and impact on logistics and investment planning.

**Crop damage** from unseasonal rainfall in 2025 was raised, with members asking whether the government has systematically assessed losses and engaged insurance mechanisms.

**District Export Hubs** were examined — how many districts have finalised export action plans and what quantum of exports has been recorded.

> "The funds owed to States under MGNREGS for wages and materials remain a persistent structural concern" — Dr. Sharmila Sarkar', '["MGNREGS Arrears", "PMGSY Roads", "Startup India", "Gati Shakti Platform", "Crop Damage Insurance"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-12-03', '## Lok Sabha — 3 December 2025

**5G rollout and rural connectivity** dominated proceedings. While the government reported that 777 of 778 districts have 5G availability, members from Tamil Nadu and Andhra Pradesh pointed out persisting black spots in rural and tribal blocks. The **4G gap in tribal areas** of Palnadu district was specifically flagged.

**Fake news and media regulation** generated sharp exchanges. Opposition members questioned whether the **Press Council of India** and the government have taken any punitive action against channels and social media accounts spreading disinformation. The Minister defended the regulatory framework while members called for stronger action.

**Public Distribution System (PDS)** in aspirational districts was assessed, with questions on per-capita allocation adequacy and last-mile delivery gaps.

**Telecommunications infrastructure** in Tamil Nadu (35,655 5G BTSs) was acknowledged, but rural Kanchipuram districts were cited as underserved.

**Parliament''s GMRT project** in Pune and its impact on local development was raised, with members seeking infrastructure compensation for affected villages.

> "There is zero data in the reply given. Can the Minister confirm whether the Government has taken even a single punitive action against major news channels spreading fake news?" — Shri B. Manickam Tagore', '["5G Rural Coverage", "Fake News Regulation", "Press Council", "PDS Aspirational Districts", "4G Tribal Areas"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-12-04', '## Lok Sabha — 4 December 2025

**Road and highway infrastructure** was the defining theme. Road Transport Minister **Shri Nitin Gadkari** took extensive questions on specific highway projects:

- The **Kozhikode–Wayanad Tunnel** in Kerala: state-funded but stalled by land acquisition costs (₹50 crore/km) and unresolved State Government commitments.
- **NH-12 through Kolkata** (formerly NH-34): Members flagged deteriorating conditions between Barajaguli and other segments.
- **Dhule–Pimpalgaon highway** work completion timelines.

**Fund utilisation delays** were a cross-cutting concern — members from Meghalaya raised the issue of Centre funds getting stuck in State treasuries before reaching students and beneficiaries.

**GMRT project** compensation discussions continued from the previous day, with members from Maharashtra pressing for a dedicated development package.

An exchange on **land acquisition and State contributions** to National Highway projects highlighted the governance friction between Centre and States, particularly on projects with high social land costs.

> "In Kerala the land acquisition cost comes to Rs. 50 crore per kilometre. The State committed to contributing 50 per cent — that commitment remains unfulfilled." — Shri Nitin Jairam Gadkari', '["Kozhikode Wayanad Tunnel", "National Highway NH-12", "Land Acquisition Cost", "Road Infrastructure Funding", "Centre-State Coordination"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-12-05', '## Lok Sabha — 5 December 2025

The sitting saw the broadest range of topics in the Winter Session, with Question Hour spanning health, agriculture, science, and ports.

**Malnutrition and child health**: Members highlighted that only ₹1.09 is allocated per newborn under current schemes, pressing the Health Minister for a comprehensive policy response. **NCD burden** (diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, cancer) was debated with calls for timely diagnostic infrastructure — especially MRI machines — in underserved districts under **PM-ABHIM**.

**Health Minister Jagat Prakash Nadda** gave a detailed defence of the government''s holistic 2017 Health Policy, covering prevention, promotion, and curative care.

**Fertiliser policy**: The differential GST treatment of conventional fertilisers (5%) versus nano liquid urea and bio-fertilisers (0%) was raised, with IFFCO''s 9 crore nano urea bottles cited as evidence of policy transition.

**Green Hydrogen Hubs**: Members asked which major ports have been identified for development as Green Hydrogen Hubs under the National Green Hydrogen Mission.

**Yoga and Naturopathy**: A proposal for an Apex Research Institute in Andhra Pradesh was debated under AYUSH.

**District hospitals to medical colleges** (PPP mode): Members sought State-wise breakdowns of upgrades underway.

> "Only Rs. 1.09 is provided for every newborn under current allocations — I urge the Minister to reconsider this budgetary commitment." — Shrimati Bag Mitali', '["Malnutrition Policy", "NCD Burden", "PM-ABHIM Diagnostics", "Green Hydrogen Hubs", "Nano Urea GST"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2025-12-08', '## Lok Sabha — 8 December 2025

**Currency and economy** opened the day, with members raising the consecutive weakening of the rupee against the dollar and seeking government intervention to manage exchange rate volatility.

**Education and PM-SHRI Schools**: Members flagged that only 12,079 schools were selected under PM-SHRI against a target of 14,500 — less than 1.5% of all government schools. Education Minister Sukanta Majumdar was pressed on district-specific data for Warangal.

**Sexual harassment at universities**: A distressing case of sexual abuse at the **National Sanskrit University** was raised, with the victim facing blackmail after reporting the assault. The member urged systemic institutional action.

**Railway infrastructure**: Members raised pending Khelo India funds (₹3.5 crore disbursed of ₹7 crore sanctioned for Thanjavur sports complex), Sabarimala and Tiruvannamalai festival train frequency, and special train operations during festive seasons.

**ESI and EPF** questions surfaced again — dual administration of ESI dispensaries between Centre and States creating accountability gaps.

> "An SC female from the National Sanskrit University was sexually abused by an Assistant Professor and then blackmailed with videos. She has given a complaint — the institution must act." — Dr. Kadiyam Kavya', '["Rupee Weakening", "PM-SHRI Schools", "Sexual Harassment Universities", "Railway Festival Trains", "Khelo India Funds"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2026-01-28', '## Lok Sabha — 28 January 2026

The joint sitting of both Houses of Parliament received the **President''s Address** — the traditional opening of the Budget Session. The Secretary-General tabled both Hindi and English versions of the Presidential Address.

The address highlighted India''s economic progress, governance reforms, and development trajectory over the past year, setting the legislative agenda for the Budget Session. Key themes flagged in the Address include continued focus on economic growth, social welfare, and national security.

This is the formal constitutional proceeding that marks the commencement of a new Parliamentary session, with substantive debate on the Address scheduled for the days that follow.', '["Presidential Address", "Budget Session 2026", "Constitutional Proceeding"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2026-01-29', '## Lok Sabha — 29 January 2026

A brief sitting primarily recording the President''s Address delivered on 28 January 2026. The Secretary-General tabled copies for the official record.

The sitting serves as procedural continuation following the joint session, with substantive parliamentary work — including debate on the Motion of Thanks for the President''s Address — scheduled for subsequent sittings.', '["Presidential Address", "Budget Session 2026"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2026-02-02', '## Lok Sabha — 2 February 2026

The Budget Session 2026 commenced in earnest with **debate on the Motion of Thanks for the President''s Address**. This brought the most charged partisan exchanges of the session.

**Leader of Opposition Shri Rahul Gandhi** delivered a major speech taking on the government''s record, raising questions about the patriotism framing used against the Congress Party and citing documentary evidence that drew protests from Treasury benches including **Shri Rajnath Singh**.

**Labour and welfare questions** opened the day — EPF scheme beneficiaries (81 lakh people), post-retirement security gaps, and the ₹75,000 crore corpus were debated, with members seeking expansion of coverage.

**ESI dispensaries**: Members raised the incomplete ESI Hospital in Thoothukkudi — 98% complete but non-operational due to absence of medical superintendent and OPD staff appointments.

**Employment Linked Incentive (ELI) Scheme**: Members from aspirational districts (including Ramanathapuram) raised awareness gaps and strict eligibility criteria excluding many workers, and sought targeted outreach.

**General Insurance and IRDAI**: Mahua Moitra raised the systemic failure of insurance regulators to resolve consumer complaints, with data showing a surge in unresolved grievances.

**CPWD school construction** in Delhi (Kalichedu and Talupur MMLWO schools) was flagged as stalled since 2023 despite approved drawings.

> "Sir, this is 100 per cent authentic" — Shri Rahul Gandhi, responding to protests from the Treasury benches during his speech on the President''s Address', '["Motion of Thanks Address", "Rahul Gandhi Speech", "ESI Hospitals", "ELI Scheme", "IRDAI Insurance Complaints"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO digests (sitting_date, digest_text, hot_topics, model_used) VALUES ('2026-02-03', '## Lok Sabha — 3 February 2026

**Agriculture** dominated the second sitting of the Budget Session, with multiple members seeking clarity on the **Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY)** — specifically on new risks covered (wild animal attacks, paddy inundation) and the farmer claim settlement mechanism.

**Organic farming in Maharashtra**: Shrimati Supriya Sule pressed the Minister on implementation of **Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY)** in Maharashtra, seeking district-level data and farmer uptake statistics.

**Free Trade Agreements**: Members raised the government''s pursuit of bilateral and multilateral FTAs — market access provisions, tariff rationalisation, and services mobility for Indian professionals.

**CAPF services and promotions**: Members from North Andhra (Vizianagaram, Srikakulam) raised infrastructure gaps for CAPF aspirants — lack of physical training facilities forcing youth to travel long distances. Slow promotions and limited senior posts in organised Group A CAPF services were flagged, with reference to Supreme Court observations.

**PMGSY Phase IV** coverage gaps: Members sought assessment of unconnected habitations in Nandurbar, Bhandara, Solapur, Sangli, and Dhule districts.

**Animal Husbandry Infrastructure Development Fund (AHIDF)** projects sanctioned in Tamil Nadu, including Tiruvannamalai and Kanchipuram, were reviewed.

> "Youth from North Andhra join BSF and CAPFs every year, but due to lack of local facilities they must travel long distances for preparation. The Government must address this." — Shri Appalanaidu Kalisetti', '["PMFBY Crop Insurance", "Organic Farming Maharashtra", "Free Trade Agreements", "CAPF Promotions", "PMGSY Phase IV"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO politician_profiles (member_id, profile_text, key_topics, model_used) VALUES (46, '## Parliamentary Profile: Shri Rahul Gandhi

**Wayanad (Kerala) · Indian National Congress · Leader of the Opposition**

Shri Rahul Gandhi is the Leader of the Opposition in the 18th Lok Sabha — the first formal LoP in a decade. Representing Wayanad, he is one of the most prominent voices on the opposition benches, leading debates on constitutional, economic, and social themes.

### Parliamentary Style
Gandhi''s speeches blend personal narrative with sharp political critique. He frequently challenges the government''s record on employment, economic inequality, and democratic institutions. His oratory ranges from sober constitutional arguments to pointed rhetorical confrontations with senior ministers.

### Key Positions
- **Economy**: Consistently raises unemployment data, wage stagnation, and the gap between macro growth metrics and household economic reality.
- **Democracy and institutions**: Questions the autonomy of regulatory bodies and investigative agencies under the current government.
- **Social justice**: Advocates for caste census, enhanced reservations in private employment, and protection of minority rights.

### Notable Speeches (18th LS)
In the February 2026 debate on the **Motion of Thanks for the President''s Address**, Gandhi cited documentary evidence against the government''s record and challenged claims about the Congress Party''s patriotism, drawing sustained protests from the Treasury benches including the Defence Minister.

> "Sir, this is 100 per cent authentic." — February 2026, responding to protests from Shri Rajnath Singh during President''s Address debate

### Topic Focus
Economic inequality, youth employment, democratic institutions, caste justice, foreign policy scrutiny', '["Economic inequality", "Youth employment", "Democratic institutions", "Caste census", "Opposition leadership"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO politician_profiles (member_id, profile_text, key_topics, model_used) VALUES (6, '## Parliamentary Profile: Shrimati Supriya Sule

**Baramati (Maharashtra) · Nationalist Congress Party (SP) · Senior Opposition Member**

Shrimati Supriya Sule represents Baramati — one of Maharashtra''s most closely watched constituencies — and brings deep expertise in agriculture, rural development, and Maharashtra-specific policy to the House.

### Parliamentary Style
Methodical and evidence-based, Sule consistently frames questions around grassroots data and farmer welfare. She is known for using Question Hour effectively to extract Ministry commitments on Maharashtra schemes.

### Key Positions
- **Agriculture**: Focuses on organic farming promotion, crop insurance coverage, and PMFBY implementation in Maharashtra.
- **Rural livelihoods**: Advocates for village-level connectivity, PMGSY coverage, and rural job guarantees under MGNREGS.
- **Women in agriculture**: Has consistently asked for disaggregated data on women farmer beneficiaries.

### Notable Parliamentary Activity (18th LS)
In February 2026, Sule raised a detailed question on the implementation of **Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY)** in Maharashtra, seeking district-level organic farming data and farmer uptake statistics — signalling her long-running advocacy for sustainable agriculture.

> "Whether the Government has taken effective steps to promote organic farming in Maharashtra through PKVY" — February 2026

### Topic Focus
Organic farming, crop insurance, Maharashtra rural development, women farmers, PMGSY connectivity', '["Organic farming", "PMFBY crop insurance", "Maharashtra agriculture", "Rural connectivity", "Women farmers"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO politician_profiles (member_id, profile_text, key_topics, model_used) VALUES (47, '## Parliamentary Profile: Shri K. C. Venugopal

**Rajya Sabha / Alappuzha (Kerala) · Indian National Congress · General Secretary**

Shri K. C. Venugopal serves as the All India Congress Committee''s General Secretary and Organisation-in-charge, making him one of the most strategically significant members on the opposition benches. He brings Kerala''s concerns to parliamentary debate while also coordinating the broader opposition strategy.

### Parliamentary Style
Venugopal is disciplined and strategic in his interventions, often raising issues that establish the Congress Party''s political record and position. His questions are typically tied to specific policy failures or Central-State coordination gaps.

### Key Positions
- **Federal relations**: Advocate for State autonomy and opposition to Centre''s unilateral policy impositions.
- **Kerala development**: Raises infrastructure and economic issues specific to Kerala, including coastal connectivity and Kerala''s public sector.
- **Democratic accountability**: Active in holding the government to account on media freedom and regulatory capture.

### Topic Focus
Federal relations, Kerala development, party organisation, democratic accountability, Central-State coordination', '["Federal relations", "Kerala development", "Democratic accountability", "Congress party organisation", "Central-State coordination"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO politician_profiles (member_id, profile_text, key_topics, model_used) VALUES (65, '## Parliamentary Profile: Prof. Sougata Ray

**Dum Dum (West Bengal) · All India Trinamool Congress · Senior Parliamentarian**

Prof. Sougata Ray is one of the most experienced members on the opposition benches, with a long parliamentary career spanning multiple terms. He represents Dum Dum in West Bengal and brings an academic''s precision to parliamentary debate.

### Parliamentary Style
Ray is known for his mastery of procedural rules and his ability to ask pointed, data-driven questions that expose policy gaps. He rarely engages in rhetorical excess, preferring to let evidence make his case.

### Key Positions
- **Labour welfare**: Particularly focused on ESI (Employees'' State Insurance) dispensary coverage in West Bengal, raising the slow pace of new dispensary approvals.
- **Workers'' rights**: Advocates for expansion of social security coverage to unorganised sector workers.
- **West Bengal development**: Consistently raises infrastructure and governance gaps affecting his constituents.

### Notable Parliamentary Activity (18th LS)
In February 2026, Ray pressed the government on whether the Ministry would confirm how many ESI dispensaries (one-doctor, two-doctor, or three-doctor models) had received approval for West Bengal — following up on in-principle approvals with implementation accountability.

> "May I know whether the Minister would be pleased to state how many such dispensaries have been allowed in West Bengal?" — February 2026

### Topic Focus
ESI and labour welfare, West Bengal infrastructure, workers'' social security, parliamentary procedure', '["ESI dispensaries", "Labour welfare", "West Bengal infrastructure", "Workers social security", "Parliamentary procedure"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO politician_profiles (member_id, profile_text, key_topics, model_used) VALUES (119, '## Parliamentary Profile: Shrimati Nirmala Sitharaman

**Rajya Sabha · Bharatiya Janata Party · Finance Minister**

Shrimati Nirmala Sitharaman serves as India''s Finance Minister — the first woman to hold this position on a full-time basis — and has been central to every major economic policy debate in the 18th Lok Sabha. She presents the Union Budget annually and defends the government''s fiscal strategy against sustained opposition scrutiny.

### Parliamentary Style
Sitharaman is methodical and data-heavy in her responses. She frequently invokes international comparisons to position India''s economic performance, and is known for detailed, lengthy replies that walk through the government''s policy rationale.

### Key Positions
- **Fiscal policy**: Oversees India''s fiscal consolidation while defending capital expenditure as a growth driver.
- **Financial sector**: Has driven significant reforms in banking, insurance, and capital markets.
- **Disinvestment**: Manages strategic disinvestment decisions including IDBI Bank.
- **GST rationalisation**: Regular clarifications and adjustments to GST structures across sectors.

### Topic Focus
Union Budget, fiscal policy, banking reforms, disinvestment, GST structure, capital markets, financial inclusion', '["Union Budget", "Fiscal policy", "Banking reforms", "IDBI disinvestment", "GST rationalisation"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO politician_profiles (member_id, profile_text, key_topics, model_used) VALUES (48, '## Parliamentary Profile: Shri B. Manickam Tagore

**Vellore (Tamil Nadu) · Indian National Congress · Whip**

Shri B. Manickam Tagore serves as the Congress Party Whip in the Lok Sabha and is a consistently vocal presence in Question Hour and Zero Hour debates. Representing Vellore, he brings Tamil Nadu''s concerns to national parliamentary debate while also playing a key opposition coordination role.

### Parliamentary Style
Tagore is combative and direct. He specialises in framing questions that expose the gap between government claims and measurable outcomes — particularly on media accountability, regulatory enforcement, and social justice.

### Key Positions
- **Media and information**: Has repeatedly asked the government to demonstrate concrete enforcement action against news channels and social media accounts spreading misinformation.
- **Employment and labour**: Raises questions on factory worker welfare and industrial dispute resolution in Tamil Nadu.
- **Social justice**: Advocates for SC/ST welfare and anti-discrimination enforcement.

### Notable Parliamentary Activity (18th LS)
In December 2025, Tagore challenged the government to name even one punitive action taken against channels spreading fake news — a sharp moment that highlighted the gap between the government''s stated commitment to media regulation and actual enforcement.

> "Sir, there is zero data in the reply given. Can the Minister confirm whether the Government has taken even a single punitive action against major news channels spreading fake news?" — December 2025

### Topic Focus
Media accountability, fake news regulation, Tamil Nadu labour welfare, SC/ST rights, democratic institutions', '["Media accountability", "Fake news regulation", "SC/ST rights", "Tamil Nadu labour", "Democratic institutions"]', 'claude-sonnet-4-6 (cowork)');
